nswer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114139}] :question/id #uuid "220bea80-64d5-518a-b644-2cf71b9bd86e" :question/math-academy-id "q-45466" :question/problem "An African reserve for elephants opened up at the beginning of the year $2015$. Due to disease, the number of elephants decreases by one-half every year and, by the beginning of the year $2018$, the number of elephants in the reserve decreased to $56 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{3}$. What does the factor $56$ mean in this context?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "972b7088-fbad-5a48-817e-11d57def70fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114536} {:answer/id #uuid "eba03512-5d0b-5e0e-bc3f-7e44197d2d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114537} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9a0537-2f88-5147-b543-d20f93369a56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114538} {:answer/id #uuid "35dcfb6c-782c-533a-9fe0-b8cf1aa2a2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114539} {:answer/id #uuid "7c45d5d0-1a63-541b-8551-d06c62b3703b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114540}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d621509b-18d1-5bcc-8083-28b5a1a0bd17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114535}] :question/id #uuid "2548b628-607a-598d-9d99-bf6c305106c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-66664" :question/problem "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y = (\\frac{1}{3})^{3x - 1} - 2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd68d2bc-d9d8-5ef1-b6d2-5b92a5dc877c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114548} {:answer/id #uuid "db7de195-c77a-577d-9201-1ca20982d49f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114549} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1ea696-f315-5201-99e4-b6fb59b32863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114550} {:answer/id #uuid "6abceb1e-5ddd-54f0-a8b2-4406ce5fc379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114551} {:answer/id #uuid "78cd62cd-3f03-56a1-aab6-a14e685be740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114552}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4b6306dc-abdc-5962-b6b0-34d1eaee6407" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47d0f8b9-d181-5339-8469-94e83ce3b394" :question/math-academy-id "q-66672" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x) = 2(\\frac{1}{3})^{2x} - 6$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b61bbffb-8e53-5408-be24-4351a2de0540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114854} {:answer/id #uuid "b64b5f3c-9c21-506d-baa7-022faf50d296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114855} {:answer/id #uuid "f72f292f-2084-5088-840a-23ff09ff90e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114856} {:answer/id #uuid "087292e2-d4ae-5020-b42d-c2b83b616329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114857} {:answer/id #uuid "65ed8e82-8f31-542c-9a78-fcaf9f12c642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114858}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4df3a8de-7d5e-509b-a743-328640e1e5b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114853}] :question/id #uuid "3a09396e-0046-540e-bf73-8f83cae9493f" :question/math-academy-id "q-92958" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x) = 5(\\frac{1}{2})^{3x} + 3$. Given that $f(x) → a$ as $x → ∞$, then $a =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119514 :question/id #uuid "42109de0-eb59-53f9-982e-1433f630b067" :question/math-academy-id "e-12568" :question/problem "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e2/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119522 :question/id #uuid "7684ddd0-1e24-57e4-a486-4cdb021a68bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-13015" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9}{64}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $a\\geq 0,$ then \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2} = a.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $9$ and $64$ are both perfect squares, where\n\n$$\n9=3^2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n64 = 8^2.\n$$\n\nSo, to work out $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9}{64}},$ we express $\\dfrac{9}{64}$ as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{64}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{8}^{2}}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{8\\cdot 8}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119525 :question/id #uuid "60f95f30-9f10-5fe5-b5b6-f7b74e219a57" :question/math-academy-id "e-13375" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{64}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $1$ and $64$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{64}},$ we express $\\dfrac{1}{64}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{64}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1}{4\\cdot 4\\cdot 4}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119532 :question/id #uuid "8cb590c5-586d-56a1-af70-d693ea055419" :question/math-academy-id "e-14996" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) =\\sin{t} \\,\\mathbf i -\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}‖i+j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)}{1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119533 :question/id #uuid "2f92f568-0bf5-58bd-87ce-e6be8d05fe69" :question/math-academy-id "e-14997" :question/problem "Given  the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\sqrt 3 t , \\:  \\cos t, \\: \\sin{t}\\right\\rangle,$ find the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve." :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119534 :question/id #uuid "f5e6498b-73f9-5760-81bb-14e9acc495f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14998" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) = \\sin{t} \\cdot \\mathbf q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j+k)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-2\\,k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119545 :question/id #uuid "86abbc30-d597-5461-9adf-dec505ebf3f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21771" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y = \\dfrac{C}{x^2},$ where $C\\neq0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y = \\dfrac{C}{x^2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{C}{x^2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nC = x^2y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{2C}{x^3} = -\\dfrac{2x^2y}{x^3}\n= -\\dfrac{2y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{2y}{x}\\right)}\n= \\dfrac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int 2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int 2y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y = \\dfrac{C}{x^2},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n2y^2 - x^2 = K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/82/82bd1506c86aff662ba47618c0a6cdaf8ed1db3fa617be301fcb2d318373a913.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\neq0,$ we have $y\\neq0$ on every curve in the family, so slopes $y'$ and $'_\\perp$ are well-defined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119558 :question/id #uuid "7b4c22d8-65f0-5a5e-9eb5-754f778a7bf7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3062" :question/problem "![](images/46/46e9f26846b088dfe225b36b889c788ed847c08376295f02dc2c9fbaf72a1645.png)\n\nFind the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane, we need to compute the position vector $\\mathbf p$ and a vector $\\mathbf n$ that is perpendicular to the osculating plane. \n\nThe position vector of the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p} = \\mathbf{r}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\left\\langle \\sin{\\left(6\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} , \\:  -\\cos{\\left(6\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)}, \\: 8\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right\\rangle  = \\left\\langle -1, 0, 2\\pi \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTo compute the perpendicular vector, we need to compute the unit tangent $\\mathbf T \\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right)$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N \\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,4⟩=\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3,4⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,0⟩=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the cross product of these two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3,4⟩\\times ⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\,j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the vector above, we can multiply it by a constant to yield a simpler vector that points in the same direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n} = 5\\cdot \\left\\langle 0 , \\dfrac{4}{5} , \\dfrac{3}{5} \\right\\rangle = \\langle 0, 4, 3  \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{|⟨-1,0,2\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,3⟩|}{‖⟨0,4,3⟩‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119561 :question/id #uuid "9b2026e3-781b-5e73-8dc2-0e8f2789fe67" :question/math-academy-id "e-3065" :question/problem "Consider the curve  $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle 2t-1 ,\\: 8t+2 ,\\: -2t \\right\\rangle, \\, t \\geq 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $\\mathbf{r}(0)$ to $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $s=6\\sqrt{2}t.$ Find $\\mathbf R(s),$ the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since  $s=6\\sqrt{2}t,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\sqrt{2}t & =s \\\\ t & =\\frac{s}{6\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ by its arc length $s,$ with $s \\geq 0,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12}) \\\\  & =⟨2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12})-1,\\,8(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12})+2,\\,-2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{12})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{6}-1,\\,\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}s}{6}+2,\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s}{6}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨\\sqrt{2}s-6,\\,4\\sqrt{2}s+12,\\,-\\sqrt{2}s⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119563 :question/id #uuid "48350682-3ad0-5311-bb47-782ec454390c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3067" :question/problem "Given the curve  $\\mathbf r(t)=\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf i - \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf j+\\sqrt{3}t\\,\\mathbf k, \\, t \\geq 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by $s=2t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $\\mathbf{T}(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=2t,$ we have that $t=\\dfrac{s}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $\\mathbf R(s)$ that parametrizes $\\mathbf r(t)$ by its arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{s}{2}k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}s}{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s,$ the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(s) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}s}\\mathbf R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}s}{2})k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\,k).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119564 :question/id #uuid "219df52e-8833-58df-8a2f-ee53cfed5640" :question/math-academy-id "e-3135" :question/problem "Consider the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(s) = -\\dfrac{4}{5}\\sin{s}\\,\\mathbf i+\\dfrac{3}{5} \\sin{s}\\, \\mathbf j + \\cos{s}\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by the arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\|\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\,j-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,j-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+0\\,j-k \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature at the point where $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖=‖-k‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119574 :question/id #uuid "3e36e916-16af-55eb-9ece-026b68eb93ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3338" :question/problem "Consider a curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $\\mathbf r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|} = \\dfrac{1}{\\:\\dfrac{1}{3}\\:} = 3\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119577 :question/id #uuid "1f374865-c7f7-510c-b361-fbad352d86c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3452" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve  $\\mathbf r(t) =  t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin t\\, \\mathbf j$ at $t = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t) = \\dfrac {|x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)|} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 0|}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119581 :question/id #uuid "94f2a6ea-7f0f-57e7-b71c-85b661f4b8fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3493" :question/problem "$\\sec(-210^\\circ) =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-210^\\circ$ that lies in the range $[0^\\circ,360^\\circ)$.\n\n$$\n-210^\\circ + 360^\\circ = 150^\\circ\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sec\\left(-210^\\circ\\right) = \\sec (150^\\circ)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $150^\\circ$ corresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos (150^\\circ) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sec (150^{∘}) &= (1)/(\\cos (150^{∘})) \\\\\n&= (1)/((-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sec \\left(-210^\\circ\\right) = -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119582 :question/id #uuid "b936fd5e-f834-5868-b204-e1eadc765765" :question/math-academy-id "e-3494" :question/problem "Determine the value of $\\tan\\left(-\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac {7\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nthat lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{7\\pi} 4 + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(-\\dfrac{7\\pi} 4\\right) = \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi} {4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$.\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (-\\frac{7π}{4}) &= \\tan (\\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= (\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}))/(\\cos (\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119607 :question/id #uuid "50685ac4-9980-5841-a669-783f6086a9dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4770" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/c2/c21c2b99cdd8480ab891a23f92687d29230da7da9de9bd75a903919e54c48a1f.svg)\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_2E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119620 :question/id #uuid "94a17888-dcef-58e0-8147-0dec3745f136" :question/math-academy-id "e-4799" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\;\\Pi \\,:\\, \\mathbf{r}=s \\mathbf{b} + t \\mathbf{c}, \\, t,s \\in \\mathbb{R},$ where  $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1, 4, 5 \\rangle, \\mathbf{c} = \\langle 2, 4, 6 \\rangle$ and  $\\mathbf{u}= \\langle 2, 10, 12 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\Pi$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s+2t &=2 \\\\ 4s+4t &=10 \\\\ 5s+6t &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s+2t &=2 \\\\ -4t &=2 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t = 2 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad t = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s+2t & =2 \\\\ s+2(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ s-1 & =2 \\\\ s & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=3, \\, t=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $\\Pi.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=3\\mathbf{b} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{c}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119633 :question/id #uuid "90f07e38-405a-5f79-8501-0b4dfb8fa4c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4825" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\mathbf{b}_3 \\},$ where\n![](images/5d/5d9d3780a503745f072cdb2e9cea0eedddca811143b05a2cc426480e93c8dac9.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We need to find $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above system as an augmented matrix $M$ and reducing using Gaussian elimination, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=7 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} &=\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 =2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-9x_2 - 2 = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2 = -1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_2=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+3(-1) = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x_1 = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/76/766d220a3c5bc2819ab05886751b6a1b782ece06c9d71b9baaaee19e9a2ecbdd.svg)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9455b7e1-8e45-5b8d-8930-b09294afde81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289026} {:answer/id #uuid "39d23c9a-dade-5d1b-902c-eabbc6bdf7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39d23c9a-dade-5d1b-902c-eabbc6bdf7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442571} :answer-field/id #uuid "b860cd5e-c7b3-588c-8a3f-8511ad544821" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbe7871a-ccc9-5a95-8370-21299423ec38" :question/math-academy-id "q-222851" :question/problem "Given  the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c09526-f582-5176-816e-4812bd5323b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117177} {:answer/id #uuid "976ad191-e6ba-5c72-ba2b-d90911a786ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117178} {:answer/id #uuid "ef33bf54-394c-5344-9fc6-e4ccb75d01a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117179} {:answer/id #uuid "2a720de5-be20-5e62-9aa2-220f6633bc08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117180} {:answer/id #uuid "6f567b3a-6e00-57be-a17d-6ffbaf3cfdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117181}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb10b42-3c3f-5cc8-b4c8-ccf734471312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117176}] :question/id #uuid "6e84cddd-2024-5b13-bcca-315f9685110a" :question/math-academy-id "q-23382" :question/problem "Determine the value of $\\cot (-\\frac{π}{3})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2104e133-914f-5ccf-9abb-bb338526ec6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300116} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7b2fae-8ac3-57a0-bffe-05751f19e1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300187} {:answer/id #uuid "9e343b3d-6257-5d65-9a7e-1c0b02359997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300760} {:answer/id #uuid "a782896a-abec-59e4-9290-6a432fe3815a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300805} {:answer/id #uuid "bebe977a-dc05-5a44-8579-fd4c6afc897a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bebe977a-dc05-5a44-8579-fd4c6afc897a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300914} :answer-field/id #uuid "38691da1-c3cd-5bcd-bc71-b36d06760bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e944b722-89ed-5df5-8b31-cb3570eaaf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32595" :question/problem "If $x=2\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(5,9).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{y})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{1/2})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1/2}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}+\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}+\\frac{3}{2y\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y+3}{2y\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{2y\\sqrt{y}}{2y+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(5,9),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(5,9)} & =\\frac{2(9)\\sqrt{9}}{2(9)+3}=\\frac{18}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d73b7d-9403-55ab-b551-e19830698e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300052} {:answer/id #uuid "6a33eeae-06c8-52d2-b05c-f45a9e998a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300498} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0ef0fa-a60f-528b-b723-df752ee1c670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300758} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d76d1c-be51-51c6-8cde-9ba71e9a668a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=9x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300855} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e2c41b-b138-5e44-b020-de7bc94b2004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=12x-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d73b7d-9403-55ab-b551-e19830698e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300052} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a3602d1-8784-558b-8e8c-d79f5c14e514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7ef2446-7ddb-53cb-83cf-b4fed6b4ea6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32596" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(9,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{y}^{2}-2y \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}-2y}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(9,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(9,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(3)^{2}-2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{12}(x-9) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{12}-\\frac{3}{4}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{12}+\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233da062-0046-5ff6-be71-aee3cc7e71f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300130} {:answer/id #uuid "95d1ecb0-3160-5adc-97f9-910e8d9c239c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{1+{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300719} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a94346-e82d-5c11-b3c7-77080761ef66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300843} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dc110a-2fc4-5a9e-a3d2-5fed13a83b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{4+4{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301179} {:answer/id #uuid "fae0bb00-a872-5722-aa1c-2b78582a5353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233da062-0046-5ff6-be71-aee3cc7e71f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300130} :answer-field/id #uuid "84d7d2bf-ce4d-5963-b71f-6932bfd6b3ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd82fd71-e11f-52b4-879b-d6f659d732bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-32705" :question/problem "If $x=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+2{y}^{2},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-2{y}^{-2}+2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{-3}+4y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}+4y \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4{y}^{4}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+4{y}^{4}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3ad161-64a2-5b71-ad24-c120e8ec6285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398741} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7e3c75-5535-5316-bea0-bf6c928449b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,j-\\frac{3}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398742} {:answer/id #uuid "6a162c78-4b2f-5cb3-bbfc-9d6ac2a6eb14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398743} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f7e9c3-d0a4-5c14-ad36-84ce7ce201f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398744} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf33e14-d217-5957-83e1-d43d4854445a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbf33e14-d217-5957-83e1-d43d4854445a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398745} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ffbf219-9fc7-55d4-a011-546b8183375e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fee02912-87a3-5a99-92ba-f8ae9f270c88" :question/math-academy-id "q-33233" :question/problem "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(0,3,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ) \\\\ T(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,3,4)$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,3,4⟩ \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+3\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components of $i$, $j$, and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t &=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t &=3 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,3,4)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k}{1}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-i)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(i\\cdot j)+\\frac{4}{5}(i\\cdot k) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e221a65-a44e-5346-8777-5df15d203193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117674} {:answer/id #uuid "3d439e47-3587-59b8-90e6-26a8e6ad90ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442572} {:answer/id #uuid "524146c4-7e77-5f6d-af6c-ffd660ac28d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442577} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c1d1e6-bb07-517c-8756-922d8ba89e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442592} {:answer/id #uuid "fbaf0be8-2245-53a4-8fe1-5686e5bfe610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "524146c4-7e77-5f6d-af6c-ffd660ac28d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442577} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aee8c82-8119-5217-bc70-4d42d2a818f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f555a45-5f75-599a-8fee-db06b9f6384d" :question/math-academy-id "q-33493" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\n \nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2})⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨0,\\,2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}[-2t⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+({t}^{2}+2)⟨0,\\,2,\\,2t⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨4t,\\,-2{t}^{2}+4,\\,4t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{((2)^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨4(2),\\,-2(2)^{2}+4,\\,4(2)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}⟨8,-4,8⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨2,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{9}‖⟨2,-1,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{9}⟨2,-1,2⟩}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2)\\cdot T(2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}⟨-2,\\,4,\\,4⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,4,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7994da-d20f-53d5-bebe-a9ebe9def829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b7994da-d20f-53d5-bebe-a9ebe9def829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309713} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffa1ffbf-bd81-589e-adfe-3ba9b8ed211b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13747a80-1def-5154-809e-6e4568d44dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-336210" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f7c87d6914b44c303fa7c67c9f5bb4908cf3e30912886d5945eddb899549032.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $9\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=9\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=9I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=9I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+9{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(9\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +9\\beta &=8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 9\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{15},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{4}{15})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}}≈0.84\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a251b1e1-e3a3-55f6-9296-0c637c1d3132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} =-\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117730} {:answer/id #uuid "ca327ec6-e1cd-5da7-9db3-b727fd810787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117731} {:answer/id #uuid "f4210914-2c65-569f-b165-004a7dad5ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117732} {:answer/id #uuid "f6362df5-c222-5682-a51f-1a6ae64df9c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117733} {:answer/id #uuid "00ebf0be-7362-5d7f-9e3f-a24b61393199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} =-\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117734}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2d4547f2-4662-5039-8ae7-31f1987ea94d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117729} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2fa608a-6018-53f7-9823-6329ba19c4cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374795} {:answer/id #uuid "f23ef8a4-3c9d-5c39-afaf-466839291d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374796} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fcb6a8-21d4-501c-9247-b5ac80b63408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374797} {:answer/id #uuid "b28b9dcf-19ff-5607-ba3e-672507c4383c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374798} {:answer/id #uuid "c21d2a10-1347-50c9-b347-73a2493aa6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f23ef8a4-3c9d-5c39-afaf-466839291d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374796} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fae7d59-179e-5ca9-9e33-4025f0e853ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e1bd6b3-a7d6-540c-a135-e33cb65aca42" :question/math-academy-id "q-336595" :question/problem "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy={C}^{2},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({C}^{2}) \\\\ y+x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined."}]
